{
  "result": "Every town should have a library of unfinished things. Not books, necessarily, but half-knitted scarves, abandoned inventions, melodies with nowhere to go, and letters that stopped just before the truth. People could borrow an ending, or leave a beginning on a shelf for someone else to find many years later.\n\nThe librarian would need unusual qualifications. Patience, certainly, and the ability to recognize when a crooked line was becoming a river. There would be no late fees. Some things take decades to finish. Other things become beautiful only after we stop insisting that they should ever be completed at all.\n\nIn a drawer near the window, a child might discover a wooden bird with one wing. She would not know its maker had given up fifty winters earlier. She might add wheels instead of feathers, and suddenly the bird would have a future nobody had thought to imagine for it.\n\nI think we mistake completion for worth too often. We praise the bridge once it reaches the opposite bank, forgetting the courage of the first plank. We admire fluent speech and overlook the brave, awkward sentence spoken by someone learning how to ask for what they need without apologizing first.\n\nSuch a library would probably be impractical. Its catalog would be chaos. Its shelves would gather dust. But on difficult afternoons, you could walk inside and find proof that being unfinished is not the same as being lost. You could take something home. You could start again from right there.",
